            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nI don't really understand the problem. Here are some statements which might clarify things:\r\n- Writing own reporter *is* the suggested way to customize how test execution information is displayed to the user.\r\n- The `gen.reporter.stdout()` reporter does quit on finish. Supplying `quit_on_finish = false` will indeed make it not quit on finish assuming the caller wants to do something else.\r\n- [Examining the state of `cases`](https://github.com/nvim-neorocks/busted-interop-examples/blob/d7daad8a564da7eea444dfd6a640db6508d18e5b/mini.test/spec/mini_tests_spec.lua#L15-L1) *is* the suggested way to extract any information about how tests were executed. This allows for much more flexibility than providing a separate API or a boolean value to, for example, `reporter.finish()`. Couple of things I'd change in the code itself:\r\n    - There is seemingly no need to track `has_fails` in `update()`. The same can be done in `finish()`.\r\n    - I'd save `cases` inside `reporter.start()`. Same way as is [done in `gen_reporter.stdout()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/691c5f38f575b572d0c857bd22c35be9a1512925/lua/mini/test.lua#L987).\r\n\r\nDoes this clarify the situation?",
